The panic that follows unplugging the printer's USB
===================================================

From /proc/last_kmsg on the Fold 5, which holds the PREVIOUS boot - the one that
died. Line numbers below are those of the full 17608-line capture, saved beside
this file as last-kmsg-previous-boot.log on the phone and on the machine that
read it.

The fault, at line 14215:

  [ 2675.823969] [1:  binder:1108_2:18591] Unable to handle kernel paging request at virtual address 1ec0000000000000
  [ 2675.823994] [1:  binder:1108_2:18591] Mem abort info:
  [ 2675.823999] [1:  binder:1108_2:18591]   ESR = 0x96000004
  [ 2675.824991] [1:  binder:1108_2:18591] pc : pm_get_wakeup_count+0x114/0x248
  [ 2675.825031] [1:  binder:1108_2:18591] x23: 0000000000000001 x22: ffffffc00b35f738 x21: ffffff894abbb600
  [ 2675.825040] [1:  binder:1108_2:18591] x20: ffffffc00b0f5838 x19: ffffffc03fb4bb44 x18: ffffffc00f92f078

The PC and the register holding the bad pointer, lines 14238-14250:
  14235:[ 2675.824970] [1:  binder:1108_2:18591] CPU: 1 PID: 18591 Comm: binder:1108_2 Tainted: G S         OE     5.15.209-g1e6986e67a48-dirt
  14238:[ 2675.824991] [1:  binder:1108_2:18591] pc : pm_get_wakeup_count+0x114/0x248
  14239:[ 2675.825003] [1:  binder:1108_2:18591] lr : pm_get_wakeup_count+0x98/0x248
  14248:[ 2675.825073] [1:  binder:1108_2:18591] x8 : 1ec0000000000000 x7 : 0000000000000000 x6 : 000000000000003f
  14457:[ 2675.826009]I[0:  binder:2717_5: 3505] CPU: 0 PID: 3505 Comm: binder:2717_5 Tainted: G S    D    OE     5.15.209-g1e6986e67a48-dirty
  14621:[ 2676.242270] [1:  binder:1108_2:18591] CPU: 1 PID: 18591 Comm: binder:1108_2 Tainted: G S    D    OE     5.15.209-g1e6986e67a48-dirt

Then the panic and what follows it:
  14383:[ 2675.825628] [1:  binder:1108_2:18591] Kernel panic - not syncing: Oops: Fatal exception
  14731:[ 2676.483049] [1:  binder:1108_2:18591] gh-watchdog hypervisor:qcom,gh-watchdog: Causing a QCOM Apps Watchdog bite!
  17496:{ 15372030 }[ ABL ] [BLDP] reboot_reason = 0x8
  17546:{ 15372549 }[ ABL ] [BLDP] reboot_reason = 0x8

Timeline of that boot, in seconds since it started:
  2112.7  first 'unexpected PWR_EVNT' from dwc3 - the port reacting to the removal
  2632.9  the last one; 1342 of them over eight and a half minutes
  2632.3  'DWC3-msm runtime idle' - the controller gives up
  2675.8  'Unable to handle kernel paging request at virtual address 1ec0000000000000'
  2675.8  'Internal error: Oops: 0000000096000004 [#1] PREEMPT SMP'
  2675.8  'pc : pm_get_wakeup_count+0x114/0x248', in a binder thread of pid 1108
  2675.8  'Kernel panic - not syncing: Oops: Fatal exception'
  2676.5  'Causing a QCOM Apps Watchdog bite!'
          firmware reboot_reason = 0x8

What it means. pm_get_wakeup_count walks the global list of wakeup sources while
holding the wakeup-source lock. Reading /sys/power/wakeup_count is something
Android's power manager does routinely, from system_server, which is the binder
thread that faulted here. Unplugging the USB device unregisters that device's
wakeup sources. When the read and the unregistration land together, the walk
follows an entry that is gone - here into 1ec0000000000000, which is not an
address in either the user or the kernel range - and a level 0 translation fault
in interrupt context is fatal.

So the unplug is the trigger and not the cause, and neither the app nor the
printer is implicated: the faulting thread is system_server's, the function is
core power management, and the kernel is Samsung's 5.15.209-g1e6986e67a48-dirty.
It is a race, which is why it does not happen every time and why the delay after
the removal varies.
